        "id": 221494,
        "name": "MigrantP",
        "username": "MigrantP",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/migrantp/{size}/1921_2.png",
        "created_at": "2018-08-31T20:28:55.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Trajector\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/48/1914_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Trajector:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The best example is Benchmark (and I suspect Shipshape works like this, too): used to be that we'd treat it as players' choice which order to activate the end of turn effects.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I'm not sure how you were playing on the tabletop, but it has <strong>always</strong> been the case that start/end of turn effects occur in the order that the cards entered play. Originally, that was the rule for all effects, but in 2013 Christopher relaxed the rule so that it only applies to start/end of turn effects, not other effects:&nbsp;<a href=\"https://greaterthangames.com/forum/topic/timing-and-order-of-operations-4347\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">https://greaterthangames.com/forum/topic/timing-and-order-of-operations-4347</a></p>",
        "post_number": 71,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:54:11.315Z",
